/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  landscape04_methane_combustion
  ==============================

  THE COMBUSTION LANDSCAPE -- CH4 in stoichiometric air (gibbs03's own feed
  and species set), the fraction of the feed carbon fully oxidised to CO2
  against temperature at 1, 10 and 100 bar, 600 to 2500 K.

  WHAT THE THREE CURVES SHOW, and it is where the approach is INDIFFERENT.
  Below about 1500 K the equilibrium is complete conversion to better than
  0.2 % (0.99996 at 600 K, 0.9987 at 1500 K and 1 bar) -- a flat line at 1
  on any axis a plot can draw -- and a 50 K approach moves it by nothing
  visible: the whole conversion-versus-temperature curve is flat, so
  a HORIZONTAL shift along it changes nothing.  Only above ~1800 K, where
  CO2 starts to dissociate to CO, does the curve acquire a slope and the
  approach a visible effect, and pressure holds the CO2 together (the
  dissociation makes moles).  This is the page's counterexample to the
  ammonia and reforming systems, where the same 50 K moves the answer by a
  great deal.

  The grid starts at 600 K rather than 300 K by measurement: on a 300 K
  start the cells at 300 and 350 K (every pressure), 400 K / 10 bar and
  500 K / 100 bar did NOT converge -- the product water is far below its
  dew point there and the gas-only Newton the kernel runs first does not
  reach a root -- and a witness that ships hatched cells at its cold end
  teaches nothing the 600 K plateau does not.  From 600 K up every cell
  converges dry (y_H2O P stays below Psat on the whole grid).

  WHY THE PRESSURES ARE 1, 10 AND 100 BAR.  `Pgrid` is `from / to / n / log`
  (GibbsMapOp.cpp reads no list form), and three log-spaced points from 1 to
  100 bar land on the decades a textbook draws.  Ideal-gas validity is the
  same caveat as nh3_equilibrium_map's: the high-pressure curve is the
  SHAPE of the pressure dependence, not a quantitative prediction.

  temperatureApproach IS DECLARED, AT ZERO.  The EduTool's slider replaces
  the NUMBER of a declared scalar and can add no key (gui/src/case/
  methodRun.ts), so the key must be here for the page to move it.  Zero is
  true equilibrium and prints nothing; a magnitude > 0 makes the run assign
  the sign at EVERY cell from the thermicity of the overall transformation
  at that cell's T (GibbsMapOp.cpp, `resolveDirection`) and print the tally.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name      burner;
        type      gibbsMap;
        elements  ( C H O N );
        species
        (
            { name CH4;   atoms ( 1 4 0 0 ); }
            { name O2;    atoms ( 0 0 2 0 ); }
            { name N2;    atoms ( 0 0 0 2 ); }
            { name CO;    atoms ( 1 0 1 0 ); }
            { name CO2;   atoms ( 1 0 2 0 ); }
            { name water; atoms ( 0 2 1 0 ); }
            { name H2;    atoms ( 0 2 0 0 ); }
        );
        feed      { CH4 1.000452;  O2 1.999852;  N2 7.519696; }
        Tgrid     { from 600; to 2500; n 39; }             // K
        Pgrid     { from 1.0e5; to 1.0e7; n 3; log true; }  // Pa: 1, 10, 100 bar
        temperatureApproach 0;                          // K, a MAGNITUDE; 0 = true equilibrium
        metric    { type elementYield; element C; product CO2; }
        anchors
        (
            { T 1800;     P 1.0e5;   }   // gibbs03's operating point
        );
        output    { file map.csv; }
    }
);
